WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.352
GROUP BY defines the grain of a summary result.

00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:08.772
With no GROUP BY, an aggregate can summarize the whole table into one row.

00:00:08.772 --> 00:00:14.297
If we group by diagnosis, the output becomes one row per diagnosis.

00:00:14.297 --> 00:00:17.147
If we group by patient and diagnosis, the result becomes more detailed:

00:00:17.147 --> 00:00:20.692
one row per patient-and-diagnosis combination.

00:00:20.692 --> 00:00:27.932
This is why adding grouping columns usually increases the number of output rows.

00:00:27.932 --> 00:00:34.047
A beginner-friendly rule is that selected columns that are not inside aggregate functions generally need to

00:00:34.047 --> 00:00:38.942
appear in GROUP BY. But the deeper rule is about meaning.

00:00:38.942 --> 00:00:45.692
Every selected non-aggregate value should be well-defined for the group represented by that output row.

00:00:45.692 --> 00:00:49.247
Before writing GROUP BY, finish the sentence: one row in my result represents one...

00:00:49.247 --> 00:00:52.432
GROUP BY changes the grain of the result.

00:00:52.432 --> 00:00:59.192
If you group by department and month, one output row represents one department-month combination.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:02.772
State that sentence before adding measures.

00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:09.336
It becomes a quick check for whether every selected non-aggregate column belongs in the grouping.
